ndamentals": {"margin": 6.31, "lean": 0.0, "environment": 8.2, "incumbency": -2.0, "money": 0.11}, "rating_margin": 5.8, "sigma_race": 6.5, "sigma_total": 7.24, "baseline_margin": 6.01, "analyst_adjustment": -1.01, "key_factors": ["Expert ratings mostly call the seat a Democratic flip, from Lean D to Safe D", "Prediction markets price Mendoza around 77-84% to win", "Mendoza has outraised incumbent Ciscomani and holds strong cash on hand", "No recent high-quality general-election polls; available surveys are old and partisan-sponsored"], "rationale": "Raters and markets converge on a Democratic edge in this even-PVI open-seat challenge, and Mendoza's fundraising edge supports that read. With no usable recent polls and an incumbent who won narrowly before, the margin stays modest and uncertainty wide.", "watch": "Any late independent polling or a shift in the national House environment could move this back toward a true tossup.", "confidence": "medium", "notes": ["No usable general-election polls; forecast rests on fundamentals and expert ratings.", "Incumbent Juan Ciscomani (R) is running."]}, "overview": "Arizona's 6th District pairs first-term incumbent Juan Ciscomani against Democratic challenger JoAnna Mendoza in an even-partisanship swing seat. Most expert raters now call the race a Democratic flip, ranging from Tossup at Cook, Inside Elections and Sabato's to Lean D or better elsewhere, and prediction markets put Mendoza's chances near 80 percent. Mendoza has raised about $7.4 million to Ciscomani's $6.2 million and trails only slightly in cash on hand, with little outside spending on either side so far.\n\nThe polling record is thin: the only surveys are months old and sponsored by partisan groups, each showing narrow Democratic leads. That leaves the forecast resting on ratings, markets and fundraising, which point to a modest Democratic edge but with real uncertainty given Ciscomani's incumbency and the seat's swing history.", "history": [{"date": "2026-09-2